<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ibn Tufayl</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1105–<a href="/years/1185">1185</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Abubacer</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ibn Tufayl was an Andalusian philosopher, physician and court official of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a>, born near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>. Serving as physician and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-yaqub-yusuf-i">Abu Yaqub Yusuf I</a>, he introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/averroes">Averroes</a> to the court and encouraged him to write his commentaries on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>. His philosophical novel Hayy ibn Yaqzan tells of a man who reaches knowledge of God through reason alone on a desert island.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ibn-tufayl.jpg" alt="Ibn Tufayl" width="320" height="343" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ibn_%E1%B9%ACufail,_Sayr_mulhimah_min_al-Sharq_wa-al-Gharb.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Abu Yaqub Yusuf and Yaqub al-Mansur patronised the philosophers <b>Ibn Tufayl</b> and Averroes (Ibn Rushd), making the court a centre of the highest medieval intellectual culture, although al-Mansur briefly disgraced Averroes in 1195 under pressure from conservative jurists.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tíjola, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Marrakech, in what is now <a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.35,-2.44&amp;year=1157" aria-label="Tíjola on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4489 819 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ibn Tufayl"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4939 1089h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4939 1089h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.35,-2.44&amp;year=1157">What was here in 1157? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
